     {
      "type": "text",
      "value": "✨ A biome is a large region with a similar climate, landscape, plants and animals."
     },
     {
      "type": "text",
      "value": "Why can places far apart still belong to the same biome?",
      "colorRole": "focus-blue",
      "align": "center"
     }
    ]
   },
   "designUnitId": "lesson-section/teaching-sequence/unit-001",
   "speakerNotes": "Say to children: A biome isn't one named place. It's a large region where the climate and landscape are similar, so similar plants and animals can live there. Tropical rainforest is one biome. Desert, tundra, grassland and different kinds of forest are biomes too. On this map, places with the same colour can be thousands of miles apart. So why might they still belong to the same biome?\n\nThe biome boundaries are broad approximations; avoid presenting them as sharp political borders.\n\nAnswer/model for this slide: Places far apart can belong to the same biome when they have a similar climate and landscape, with similar plants and animals."
  },
